restrictToInvCurves -- computes the restrictions of a toric vector bundle to the torus invariant curves
*******************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        l = restrictToInvCurves E 
        b = isNef E 
        b = isAmple E
  * Inputs:
      * E, a *note vector bundle on a toric variety (Klyachko's description):
        (ToricVectorBundles)ToricVectorBundleKlyachko,
  * Outputs:
      * l, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,
      * b, a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,
  * Consequences:
      * The result of restrictToInvCurves will be stored as a cacheValue
        (missing documentation) in E.cache#restrictionsToInvCurves. It will be
        used by other methods.

Description
===========

Given a toric vector bundle in Klyachko's description, restrictToInvCurves
computes its restrictions to the torus invariant curves, which are isomorphic
to a direct sum of line bundles $\mathbb P^1$. Recall that on $\mathbb P^1$ any
vector bundle splits into $\oplus_i O_{\mathbb P^1}(a_i)$. Therefore
restrictToInvCurves returns a hash table with keys the invariant curves and as
values lists with the integers $a_i$.
By [HMP, Theorem 2.1], if all these integers$a_i$ are non-negative or positive,
the original toric vector bundle is nef or ample. Hence, the methods isNef and
isAmple check exactly that.
restrictToInvCurves calls internally the method *note toricChernCharacter:
toricChernCharacter,; whereas isNef and isAmple are simple checks on the output
of restrictToInvCurves.
+--------------------------------------------+
|i1 : E = tangentBundle(projectiveSpaceFan 2)|
|                                            |
|o1 = {dimension of the variety => 2 }       |
|      number of affine charts => 3          |
|      number of rays => 3                   |
|      rank of the vector bundle => 2        |
|                                            |
|o1 : ToricVectorBundleKlyachko              |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i2 : restrictToInvCurves E                  |
|                                            |
|o2 = HashTable{| -1 | => {2, 1}}            |
|               | -1 |                       |
|               | 0 | => {1, 2}              |
|               | 1 |                        |
|               | 1 | => {2, 1}              |
|               | 0 |                        |
|                                            |
|o2 : HashTable                              |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i3 : isNef E                                |
|                                            |
|o3 = true                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isAmple E                              |
|                                            |
|o4 = true                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
In this example we see that the vector bundle is ample, as all integers are
positive.

Caveat
======

These methods work for toric vector bundles on a complete simplicial toric
variety.

See also
========

  * *note ToricVectorBundleKlyachko:
    (ToricVectorBundles)ToricVectorBundleKlyachko, -- the class of all toric
    vector bundles in Klyachko's description
  * *note parliament: parliament, -- computes the parliament of polytopes to a
    toric vector bundle
  * *note compatibleBases: compatibleBases, -- computes compatible bases for a
    toric vector bundle
  * *note toricChernCharacter: toricChernCharacter, -- computes the toric Chern
    character of a toric vector bundle

Ways to use restrictToInvCurves:
================================

  * "restrictToInvCurves(ToricVectorBundleKlyachko)"

